e_shrhldr_yield":0.12475944794189844,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":100.0,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.0725584532813125,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":100.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.022460351478782684,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":62.6,"_gate_insider_own":0.0061500003,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":4.1,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Equitable Holdings, Inc., together with its consolidated subsidiaries, operates as a diversified financial services company worldwide. The company operates through six segments: Individual Retirement, Group Retirement, Asset Management, Protection Solutions, Wealth Management, and Legacy. The Individual Retirement variable annuity products, including structured capital strategies, retirement cornerstone, and investment edge primarily to affluent and high net worth individuals. The Group Retirement provides tax-deferred investment and retirement services or products to plans sponsored by educational entities, municipalities, and not-for-profit entities, as well as small and medium-sized businesses. It offers guaranteed and structured investment option, and personal income benefit variable annuity products and open architecture mutual fund platform. The Asset Management segment offers investment management and related services to various clients through institutions, retail, and private wealth management. The Protection Solutions segment provides life insurance products, such as VUL and COLI insurance, IUL insurance, and term life; and employee benefits business which includes group life, supplemental life, dental, vision, short-term disability, long-term disability, critical illness, accident and hospital indemnity insurance products to small and medium-sized businesses. The Wealth Management segment offers discretionary and non-discretionary investment advisory accounts, financial planning and advice, life insurance, and annuity products. The Legacy segment consists of the capital intensive fixed-rate GMxB business that includes ROP death benefits. The company was formerly known as AXA Equitable Holdings, Inc. and changed its name to Equitable Holdings, Inc. in January 2020. Equitable Holdings, Inc. was founded in 1859 and is based in New York, New York.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Mark  Pearson FCCA | President, CEO & Director | Age 67 | Compensation: $4.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 8K people. Revenue per employee of $1.5M ranks in the top quartile among Financial Services peers. Stock-based compensation of $33K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Equitable Holdings, Inc. is a large-cap financial services business with a $14.1B market cap on $11.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -6% over the past three years, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 0% against a 10% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","On the balance sheet, thin -3.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 12.5% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.1) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Revenue growth in the bottom decile of Financial Services peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"red","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 37% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $7.4M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Value-trap profile (score 57/100): leveraged balance sheet under pressure; durably derated by the market \u2014 cheapness may reflect deterioration, not mispricing","sev":"amber","cat":"value_trap"},{"text":"ROIC sits in the bottom quartile among Financial Services peers \u2014 no moat advantage to speak of","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Valued at a 85% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Critical Review: Equitable (NYSE:EQH) & Evertec (NYSE:EVTC)","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/06/critical-review-equitable-nyseeqh-evertec-nyseevtc.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793981747.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.846,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Critical Analysis: Equitable (NYSE:EQH) and Bitcoin Depot (NASDAQ:BTM)","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/06/critical-analysis-equitable-nyseeqh-and-bitcoin-depot-nasdaqbtm.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":17